1
00:00:00,180 --> 00:00:05,099
Turn your agent’s analysis into an interactive page you can share.

2
00:00:05,099 --> 00:00:06,888
That’s what publish-html does.

3
00:00:07,818 --> 00:00:14,739
Give your agent the content, then ask: use publish-html to make this interactive and put it on my site.

4
00:00:15,669 --> 00:00:25,318
The agent chooses a layout for the material, with system diagrams, flows, charts, or other widgets where they make the explanation easier to understand.

5
00:00:26,249 --> 00:00:30,883
Readers can follow a process, compare different views, or explore the data.

6
00:00:30,883 --> 00:00:33,972
Each interaction should help them answer a question.

7
00:00:34,902 --> 00:00:37,662
The skill publishes to your GitHub Pages repository.

8
00:00:37,662 --> 00:00:42,837
It can set one up on first use, and remembers it for the next page.

9
00:00:43,767 --> 00:00:46,110
Each document gets its own folder.

10
00:00:46,110 --> 00:00:50,796
Updates keep the same public link, while Git preserves the earlier versions.

11
00:00:51,727 --> 00:00:52,611
Want feedback?

12
00:00:52,611 --> 00:00:54,379
Enable optional page comments.

13
00:00:54,379 --> 00:01:01,010
Readers can leave suggestions, and your agent can read them, reply, and revise the page.

14
00:01:01,939 --> 00:01:07,094
It’s a standalone skill with shared scripts for Claude Code and Codex.

15
00:01:07,094 --> 00:01:12,678
The pages use plain HTML, CSS, and JavaScript, with no site build step.

16
00:01:13,608 --> 00:01:14,773
Bring your content.

17
00:01:14,773 --> 00:01:15,938
Ask for publish-html.

18
00:01:15,938 --> 00:01:20,211
Get a page people can explore—and a link you can share.
